ying the repo, then clean it up. The prompt says test script should be idempotent and use absolute paths. A test script that writes a temp file into `internal/runtime/` and then deletes it is risky (could leave residue if interrupted, and could break parallel runs). I'll avoid that.\n\nAlternative: Use `go run` with a helper program in a temp dir that imports the module via replace. Let me sketch:\n\n```go\n// main.go in temp dir\npackage main\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/runtime\"\n)\nfunc main() {\n    _, err := runtime.FindBinary(nil)\n    if err == nil { fmt.Println(\"NO_ERROR\"); return }\n    fmt.Println(err.Error())\n}\n```\n\nAnd a go.mod:\n```\nmodule checkfindbinary\ngo 1.25.0\nrequire github.com/sunquan/rick v0.0.0\nreplace github.com/sunquan/rick => /abs/path/to/rick\n```\n\nThen `go run .` with env `RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR=<empty temp>` and `PATH=<empty>` (or PATH without pi). But `go run` itself needs to find `go` toolchain in PATH. If I set PATH to empty, `go` won't be found. I need to keep `go` accessible. Hmm. I can set PATH to a dir containing just the `go` binary symlink? Or invoke `go` with absolute path and set PATH minimal for the *child* process.\n\nActually, FindBinary does `exec.LookPath(\"pi\")`. To make LookPath(\"pi\") fail, PATH must not contain pi. But I still need to run `go run`. I can:\n- Find the go binary path first (`shutil.which('go')`).\n- Run go with env where PATH is set to a directory that contains `go` (symlink) but NOT pi, and RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR set to empty temp.\n\nBut wait, does `go run`/`go build` require other PATH tools (like `git`, `gcc`, etc.)? `go run` of a simple program with no external deps (only stdlib + the module under test which has cobra/goldmark deps...). Hmm, the runtime package imports `internal/config`, `internal/agent`, and stdlib + maybe cobra? No — runtime package imports config and agent and stdlib (os/exec, etc.). config imports stdlib only. agent imports time. So the dependency graph for the helper program is: runtime → config, agent → stdlib only. No cobra. So `go run` should be able to compile with just the Go toolchain and the module cache.\n\nActually, `go run` with a replace directive needs to download the module's dependencies? The module `github.com/sunquan/rick` has go.sum with cobra/goldmark, but the helper program only imports `internal/runtime`, so only stdlib is needed for the build. go might still try to verify go.sum? With replace to local dir, it uses the local module. Should be fine.\n\nBut there's complexity: setting up a temp go.mod with replace. This is doable but adds fragility. Also, running `go run` might be slow (compilation). But it's a one-time check.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: verify FindBinary behavior by checking the source code contains the error message AND the resolution order (cfg.PiPath → RuntimeBin → LookPath(\"pi\")), and rely on the migrated Go unit tests passing. Given the acceptance says \"返回 error 含 `pi binary not found`\", grepping the source for that exact message is a reasonable proxy but weaker than behavioral.\n\nActually, I realize there might already be a Go test in cli_test.go for FindBinary. Let me read cli_test.go and agentdir_test.go to see what tests exist."}}
