{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Good. `executor_realpi_smoke_test.go` is gated behind `realpi` build tag (not run by default). `executor_realds_test.go` runs a parser-only test (no real pi). `executor_e2e_test.go` uses a mock pi binary, should be fast.\n\nThe e2e test imports `internal/actpath` and `internal/agent`. After migration to `internal/runtime`, this e2e test would still import actpath/agent (retained). Good.\n\nNow let me write the test script. Let me carefully construct it.\n\nLet me also verify: does `internal/cmd/tools_test.go` (which tests FindBinary) get run by `go test ./internal/cmd/...`? Yes, but I'm NOT running cmd tests (to avoid the hang). So I won't rely on it. My compile check `go build ./...` won't run tests.\n\nActually, wait — I realize I should double check whether the plan's KR5 requires cmd tests green, and whether the test method's normal path omits it. The prompt's 测试方法 normal path is authoritative for THIS test script: `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick && go test ./internal/runtime/... -v`. It does NOT mention cmd tests. So I'll follow it. I'll add `go build ./...` as a compile-only check (fast, no hang) to cover \"import migration doesn't break cmd/executor/actpath\".\n\nNow let me write the script.\n\nLet me structure it clearly with functions:\n- find_repo_root\n- read_text(path)\n- run(cmd, cwd, timeout)\n- glob_go_files(dir) -> list of .go files\n- contains_any(paths, needle)\n\nActually I'll keep it straightforward and readable.\n\nLet me write the full script now.\n\nKey checks list (I'll number them in comments):\n\n1. go tool available\n2. build `./cmd/rick` to temp\n3. go test `./internal/runtime/...`\n4. go build `./...` (compile all, incl cmd import migration)\n5. internal/runtime dir + migrated source files (cli.go, executor.go, agentdir.go)\n6. internal/runtime test files present (cli_test.go, executor_test.go, agentdir_test.go)\n7. old internal/agent/piagent has no .go files\n8. grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/ → no matches\n9. internal/agent interface.go retained (AgentExecutor + AgentSession)\n10. internal/actpath retained (generator.go)\n11. runtime implements agent interface (imports internal/agent, references agent.AgentSession, has NewExecutor/Execute)\n12. isSessionReady defined in runtime source + referenced in runtime tests\n13. migrated NoAgentSettled test (agent_settled fixture) present — actually check \"agent_settled\" in runtime test files and \"NoAgentSettled\" or \"isSessionReady\"\n14. Runtime interface + piRuntime + Trace (type Runtime interface, Name() string, methodText, promptFile string, *config.Config, *Trace, piRuntime, type Trace struct)\n15. --append-system-prompt in runtime source\n16. config Runtime field (json:\"runtime\" in config.go)\n17. GetDefaultConfig Runtime:\"pi\" (regex Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\" in loader.go)\n18. LoadConfig normalization (regex Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\" in loader.go)\n19. TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault in config tests\n20. FindBinary error message \"pi binary not found\" in runtime source\n21. FindBinary fallback test migrated (TestFindBinary_FallsBackToPathLookup in runtime tests)\n\nThat's comprehensive. But I need to be careful: too many checks could make the script brittle or produce many errors when unimplemented (which is fine for RED). But when the implementer follows the spec, all should pass.\n\nLet me reduce brittleness where possible but keep coverage. I'll group some checks.\n\nLet me reconsider check 13 and 21 — checking for specific test function names in migrated tests. These test names should be preserved during migration (the task says tests migrate with package/import path changes, not renamed). So `TestParseStream_NoAgentSettled` and `TestFindBinary_FallsBackToPathLookup` should exist in runtime tests. I'll check for their presence. Good.\n\nAlso check `TestParseStream_BasicSession` (migrated executor test) presence to ensure executor tests migrated. And the isSessionReady test — the implementer will ADD a new test (e.g., `TestIsSessionReady...`). I'll grep runtime tests for \"isSessionReady\" (lowercase function name). The new test likely has name `TestIsSessionReady` or `TestIsSessionReady_Settled`. I'll check \"isSessionReady\" appears in test files.\n\nLet me also verify the source defines the function as `func isSessionReady(`. I'll grep runtime non-test files for \"isSessionReady\".\n\nNow, potential issue: `isSessionReady` might be defined to take a `*piSession` and check `s.sessionID != \"\" && s.settled`. The function name is exactly `isSessionReady` per spec. Good.\n\nFor the Runtime interface `Run` signature, the spec says `Run(methodText string, promptFile string, cfg *config.Config) (sessionID string, trace *Trace, err error)`. I'll check \"methodText string\", \"promptFile string\", \"cfg *config.Config\", \"*Trace\", \"type Runtime interface\", \"Name() string\". These are all present if spec is followed.\n\nLet me write it now.\n\nFor the grep no-residual check, I'll run `grep -rn` with the repo internal dir. Actually, to be safe against grep not being found (unlikely), I'll use Python to search. But spec says grep. I'll use subprocess grep with cwd=repo_root and path \"internal/\". If returncode 0 → matches. If 1 → clean. If 2 → error (shouldn't happen). I'll capture output and include first few lines in error.\n\nNow about output: only `print(json.dumps(result))`. Debug info to stderr via `sys.stderr.write`.\n\nLet me write the script with all this.\n\nAlso need `#!/usr/bin/env python3` shebang and executable bit (chmod +x).\n\nLet me now write the file.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the build output path. I'll use `tempfile.mkdtemp()` and build to `<tmpdir>/rick`. After checks, I can leave it (temp dir auto-cleaned by OS eventually) or `shutil.rmtree`. I'll remove it in a finally to be tidy. Actually not strictly necessary; temp dirs are in /tmp. I'll keep it simple and not bo