ment check still applies. Good.\n\nAlso, the `runtime_src` vs `runtime_test` classification: `executor_realpi_smoke_test.go` ends with `_test.go` so it's classified as test. `executor_realds_test.go` ends with `_test.go`. `executor_e2e_test.go` ends with `_test.go`. All good.\n\nNow let me also reconsider: will the migrated `executor.go` still contain `func NewExecutor`? Yes, it's migrated as-is (package name changes to runtime, import path internal/runtime). `func NewExecutor(piPath string, extraArgs ...string) *Executor` → still there. Good.\n\nWill `agent.AgentSession` appear in runtime_src_txt? Yes, executor.go has `func (e *Executor) Execute(...) (agent.AgentSession, error)` and piSession methods return agent.ToolCall etc. And import `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent`. Good.\n\nWill `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent` appear? Yes, import. Good.\n\nNow `type Runtime interface` — the implementer will add a new file (e.g., runtime.go or seam.go) defining:\n```go\ntype Runtime interface {\n    Name() string\n    Run(methodText string, promptFile string, cfg *config.Config) (sessionID string, trace *Trace, err error)\n}\n```\nSo `type Runtime interface`, `Name() string`, `methodText string`, `promptFile string`, `cfg *config.Config`, `*Trace` all appear. Good.\n\n`piRuntime` — a type/struct defined. `type Trace struct` — defined. Good.\n\n`--append-system-prompt` — appears in piRuntime.Run implementation. Good.\n\nConfig:\n- `json:\"runtime\"` in config.go — yes after adding field `Runtime string json:\"runtime\"`.\n- `Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\"` in loader.go GetDefaultConfig — yes.\n- `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` in loader.go LoadConfig normalization — yes (e.g., `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"`).\n\nWait, could the normalization be written as `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"`? The regex `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` matches `Runtime = \"pi\"` in `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"`. Yes. Could it be `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"` with the assignment inside an if? Yes still matches.\n\nBut what if the implementer writes normalization differently, e.g., `if cfg.Runtime == \"\" { cfg.Runtime = DefaultRuntime }` where `DefaultRuntime = \"pi\"` constant? Then `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` would NOT match (it'd be `Runtime = DefaultRuntime`). Hmm. The spec says \"「runtime 空值 → \"pi\"」归一化落在 LoadConfig\". The most natural Go is `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"`. I'll keep the regex but maybe also add a fallback: check loader.go contains `Runtime` and `\"pi\"` near each other. Actually, to be safe, let me make the normalization check more lenient: check that loader.go contains `Runtime` at all (indicating the field is referenced in loader) AND contains a `\"pi\"` literal. But `\"pi\"` appears in loader.go already? Let me check current loader.go for `\"pi\"`. Current loader.go has no \"pi\" literal (PiPath: \"\" empty, DefaultWorkspace uses rickDirName). Let me verify: current loader.go strings: \"failed to get home directory\", \".rick\", \".rick_dev\", \"config.json\", etc. No \"pi\". So after adding Runtime:\"pi\", `\"pi\"` appears. And normalization adds another `\"pi\"`.\n\nI think a good robust check: \n- `re.search(r'Runtime\\s*[:=]\\s*\"pi\"', loader_txt)` — matches either `Runtime: \"pi\"` or `Runtime = \"pi\"`. This covers both GetDefaultConfig (colon) and normalization (equals). But I have them as two separate checks for clarity. \n\nActually, I could keep two checks but make each slightly lenient:\n- Default: `Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\"` (GetDefaultConfig field literal).\n- Normalization: check for the pattern `Runtime\\s*==\\s*\"\"` (the empty-check) OR `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"`. \n\nThe spec says normalization is \"空值 → pi\". The implementer might write `if cfg.Runtime == \"\" { cfg.Runtime = \"pi\" }`. So both `Runtime == \"\"` and `Runtime = \"pi\"` present. My current check `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` requires the assignment literal \"pi\". If they use a constant, it'd miss. To be safe, I'll change the normalization check to require `Runtime` is referenced in LoadConfig with an empty check and/or assignment. But honestly, the spec explicitly says default \"pi\" and normalization to \"pi\", so a literal \"pi\" assignment is near-certain.\n\nI'll keep it but relax: normalization check = `re.search(r'Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"', loader_txt)` OR (`'Runtime' in loader_txt` AND `'cfg.Runtime' in loader_txt`). Hmm, this gets complicated. Let me keep the simple regex for `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` and ALSO accept if loader contains `Runtime` and `\"pi\"` as a weak fallback? No, that's too weak (GetDefaultConfig alone would satisfy it).\n\nActually, let me reconsider: what's the risk of false failure? If the implementer writes normalization as `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"` (99% likely), my regex passes. If they use a named constant `DefaultRuntime = \"pi\"` and `cfg.Runtime = DefaultRuntime`, my regex fails on the normalization check → false negative. This is a plausible but unlikely variation.\n\nI'll make the normalization check a bit more lenient: accept either `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` OR (loader contains `cfg.Runtime` AND loader contains a `Runtime` empty-check `Runtime\\s*==\\s*\"\"`). Let me implement that.\n\nActually, simpler and robust: the key acceptance is \"empty config → cfg.Runtime == pi (normalized)\". This is verified by the `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault` unit test (which I already check exists and which runs via... wait, I don't run config tests). Hmm. Actually I check `RuntimeDefault` string exists in config tests, but I don't run `go test ./internal/config/...`. Should I run config tests to verify the normalization behavior actually works?\n\nLet me add `go test ./internal/config/...` to actually verify `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault` passes (real behavior). That's more faithful than just grepping the test name. The config tests are fast (no pi). Let me add that check. It also runs `TestGetDefaultConfig` etc. This directly verifies the boundary acceptance \"TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault: 空 config → cfg.Runtime == pi\".\n\nSimilarly, for isSessionReady behavior, `go test ./internal/runtime/...` runs the isSessionReady unit test. Good.\n\nSo I'll add `go test ./internal/config/...` (with timeout). And I can keep the static checks as supplementary.\n\nFor the normalization static check, I'll keep it but make lenient enough. Let me think about what's cleanest:\n\nI'll check:\n1. config.go has `json:\"runtime\"` field.\n2. loader.go `GetDefaultConfig` returns Runtime:\"pi\" → regex `Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\"`.\n3. loader.go normalization: check `cfg.Runtime` appears (indicating LoadConfig touches the field) — but that alone doesn't prove normalization. Combined with running `go test ./internal/config/...` which runs `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault`, the behavior is verified. So for the static check, I can relax to: loader.go contains `Runtime` (field referenced) and the runtime default \"pi\" literal is set somewhere (already checked via regex #2). Actually the normalization behavior is fully covered by the Go test. So maybe I should drop the brittle `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` regex and instead rely on `go test ./internal/config/...` + `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault` existence.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault` in the plan says \"空 config → cfg.Runtime == pi (归一化)\". This test would override getConfigPath to a temp file with empty JSON `{}`, call LoadConfig, and assert cfg.Runtime == \"pi\". If the implementer's normalization is in LoadConfig, this test passes. If they only set GetDefaultConfig but NOT normalize in LoadConfig, then `{}` → json.Unmarshal → Runtime stays \"\" → test fails. So `go test ./internal/config/...` passing `TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault` verifies normalization. \n\nSo my plan:\n- Keep static check: config.go has field; loader.go GetDefaultConfig Runtime:\"pi\".\n- Keep static check: `RuntimeDefault` test name exists.\n- ADD: run `go test ./internal/config/...` and require it passes (this runs TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault + all config tests).\n- For normalization static: I'll keep a lenient check that loader.go references `Runtime` beyond the struct field (i.e., `cfg.Runtime` or a normalization). Actually, let me simplify: replace the brittle `Runtime\\s*=\\s*\"pi\"` regex with checking that loader.go contains `Runtime` AND (optionally) `cfg.Runtime` or the empty-string normalization. But since the Go test now covers it, I can drop the strict normalization regex and just keep a light check.\n\nLet me reconsider to avoid over-engineering. I'll do:\n- config.go: `json:\"runtime\"` present (field).\n- loader.go: `Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\"` present (GetDefaultConfig default).\n- loader.go: `cfg.Runtime` present (LoadConfig normalization touches field) — lenient.\n- config tests: `RuntimeDefault` present.\n- RUN `go test ./internal/config/...` (verifies actual normalization behavior).\n\nThis is robust and non-brittle. Let me update.\n\nActually, wait — is `cfg.Runtime` the right variable name in LoadConfig? In LoadConfig, the loaded config is `var cfg Config` then `return &cfg`. So normalization would be `cfg.Runtime = \"pi\"`. Yes `cfg.Runtime` appears. Good.\n\nBut hold on, there's another subtlety: LoadConfig has two branches — the \"file not exists\" branch returns `GetDefaultConfig()` (which already has Runtime:\"pi\"), and the \"file exists\" branch unmarshals into cfg and returns &cfg (needs normalization). The normalization should be applied to the unmarshal branch. The implementer might apply it at the end of LoadConfig (after both branches) or only in the unmarshal branch. Either way `cfg.Runtime` appears. Good.\n\nLet me now update the script:\n1. Add `go test ./internal/config/...` run.\n2. Replace normalization regex with `cfg.Runtime` presence check (or keep both regex and lenient). I'll do: check `cfg.Runtime` in loader_txt.\n\nActually, let me keep it simple and informative:\n- Default: regex `Runtime\\s*:\\s*\"pi\"`.\n- Normalization: check `'cfg.Runtime' in loader_txt` — if absent, error \"LoadConfig() missing runtime normalization reference (cfg.Runtime)\".\n\nAnd add the go test for config.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should I add `go test ./internal/config/...`? It's fast. But it also runs `TestGetConfigPath` which checks path starts with $HOME. That's fine. And `TestLoadConfigFileNotExists` etc. All fast. Let me add it.\n\nNow let me also double check: my `go test ./internal/runtime/...` currently, with the realpi build tag, won't run realpi. Good.\n\nLet me also reconsider the `--append-system-prompt` check: it's a key result but if the implementer uses a different flag name? The spec explicitly says `--append-system-prompt`. Keep it.\n\nAlso the `methodText string` check: the spec signature is `Run(methodText string, promptFile string, cfg *config.Config)`. The parameter names are methodText and promptFile. Keep.\n\nNow let me update the script with these refinements.\n\nAlso, I realize I should double check: does `go test ./internal/config/...` currently pass (so that after my change, the \"RED\" output doesn't get confused by a config test failure unrelated to task4)? Let me run it quickly to see current state.\n\nActually currently config has no Runtime field, and no TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault, but `go test ./internal/config/...` should pass currently (all existing config tests pass). Let me verify.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_89sPsYc1wNTFKSLPq47j6969","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && go test ./internal/config/... 2>&1 | tail -5; echo \"exit=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":980,"output":4851,"cacheRead":88320,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4764,"totalTokens":94151,"cost":{"input":0.0004263,"output":0.00422037,"cacheRead":0.00032016,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00496683}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786901712588,"responseId":"9bbd45f3-56a4-4ec5-9ee1-3eb72a1a0abe","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResu