{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The e2e test uses `NewExecutor(mockPi)` with a mock path, so it's isolated. The mock pi binary uses `cat` — but the mock binary's script is `#!/bin/sh\\ncat <<'EOF'...`. When `Execute` runs it via `exec.Command(piBin, args...)`, the PATH must include `cat`. The test uses `t.TempDir()` for mockPi but doesn't change PATH, so the mock script inherits the ambient PATH (which has /usr/bin with cat). Fine. And RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR isn't set, but since piPath=mockPi is passed directly, `FindBinary` isn't invoked; Execute uses e.piPath directly. So no isolation needed. Good.\n\nSo `go test ./internal/runtime/...` will run: cli tests, agentdir tests, executor tests, e2e tests, realds tests (parser-only). realpi is gated. All should be fast and pass after migration.\n\nOne concern: `executor_realds_test.go` currently is `package piagent` with no build tag. It runs a parser test on a captured real deepseek stream. After migration it's `package runtime`. Fine.\n\nNow, another important check: my script's `go test ./internal/runtime/...` uses `-timeout 180s`. If a migrated test hangs (shouldn't), it'll fail. Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the `grep -rn 'internal/agent/piagent' internal/` check. After migration, the only potential residual is the realpi smoke comment. Let me verify there are no OTHER occurrences of \"internal/agent/piagent\" I might've missed that are in test fixture data or elsewhere. From the earlier full grep (`grep -rn \"piagent\" internal/`), the only full-path occurrences are the imports and that one comment. The `piagent.` references (comments) don't match \"internal/agent/piagent\". Good.\n\nBut wait — will the migrated `executor_realpi_smoke_test.go` comment still say `./internal/agent/piagent/...`? The implementer, following KR3 \"所有 internal/agent/piagent import 改为 internal/runtime\", focuses on imports. The comment is not an import. So there's a real risk the implementer leaves that comment. Then my grep would fail → false negative.\n\nLet me weigh: The test method's acceptance is literally `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/` 无残留. A comment containing that string IS residue. If the implementer is careful (and the task is about meticulous migration), they'd update it. But it's a judgment call.\n\nTo be safe and avoid a false failure on a cosmetic comment, I could refine the grep to target import paths only: search for `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"` (the quoted import). That's the actual thing that must be eliminated for code correctness. The comment `./internal/agent/piagent/...` is a relative path in a doc comment, not an import.\n\nHmm, but the acceptance says grep the string \"internal/agent/piagent\". Let me reconsider the spirit: \"无残留\" means no leftover references to the old package that would indicate incomplete migration. An import is code; a comment is cosmetic. The risk of false failure vs. false pass:\n\n- If I grep the bare string and the implementer leaves the comment → my test fails (false negative), even though code is correct. This could cause a correct implementation to be rejected.\n- If I grep only the import path and the implementer leaves the comment → my test passes (correct).\n\nI think the more robust choice is to check for the *import path* (quoted string) as the primary \"no residue\" check, AND separately check that `internal/runtime` is now imported where needed. But the acceptance explicitly gives the grep command. \n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I should do BOTH but treat them appropriately:\n1. Check no quoted import `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"` remains (this is the hard requirement — code must not import old package). This is the critical check.\n2. The bare-string comment occurrence: I could also flag it, but risk false negative.\n\nGiven the task is evaluated by running this script, and the goal is a correct, non-brittle test, I'll check for the quoted import path as the \"no residue\" hard check, plus also check that no .go file under internal/runtime contains the stale `./internal/agent/piagent/...` comment? That's too picky.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The grader will run my script after the implementer does task4. If my script is too strict and fails on a cosmetic comment, that's bad. If my script is too lenient and passes despite real issues, that's also bad. The quoted import path check is the right balance: it catches the real problem (code still importing old package) without failing on comments.\n\nBut the spec literally says `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/`. Hmm. Let me think about what the implementer will actually do. The implementer is an AI following the task4.md plan. Task4.md KR3 says \"所有 `internal/agent/piagent` import 改为 `internal/runtime`\". The test method says grep no residue. A careful implementer would also update comments. But there's ambiguity.\n\nI'll go with a two-tier approach to be safe:\n- Primary check (hard): no quoted import `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"` remains anywhere under internal/. This is the correctness check.\n- Keep the literal `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/` but... \n\nActually, let me reconsider. I'll check the quoted import path for the hard failure. I'll ALSO run the literal grep, but instead of failing hard on comment-only matches, I'll only report an error if there are matches that are NOT just the known comment. That's complex.\n\nSimplest robust approach: grep for the quoted import path `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"`. If found → error (import not migrated). This directly verifies KR3 and the spirit of \"无残留\". I'll drop the bare-string grep to avoid the cosmetic comment false-positive.\n\nBut wait, the acceptance criteria in the prompt's test method explicitly says `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/` 无残留. If I deviate, am I not following the test method? The prompt also says \"根据上面的'测试方法'实现每个测试步骤\". \n\nHmm. Let me re-read the exact test method text: \"另 `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/` 无残留（但 `internal/agent` 接口仍存在）\".\n\nI think the cleanest faithful implementation is to actually run `grep -rn \"internal/agent/piagent\" internal/` and require no matches. The implementer, to pass this exact command, must remove ALL occurrences including the comment. This is the literal acceptance. And honestly, a comment saying `go test -tags realpi ./internal/agent/piagent/...` in a file now located in `internal/runtime` IS wrong/misleading and should be fixed during migration. So requiring it is defensible.\n\nBut there's a real risk the implementer (another AI) misses the comment. If they do, my test correctly flags a minor residue. Is that acceptable? The acceptance says no residue, so flagging is correct per spec. However, as the test author, I want to avoid false negatives that reject otherwise-correct work.\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle: What would a careful human reviewer do? They'd note the comment is stale and want it fixed, but wouldn't reject the whole task for it. But the acceptance is binary (pass/fail).\n\nI'll make a pragmatic decision: keep the literal grep as a hard check (faithful to spec), because:\n1. It's exactly what the acceptance says.\n2. The comment is genuinely stale and should be updated — it's a one-line fix.\n3. The task4 plan explicitly says \"所有 internal/agent/piagent import 改为 internal/runtime\" and the realpi smoke comment references the old path; a thorough migration updates it.\n\nActually, wait — I want to reconsider whether the realpi smoke comment even matters for the grep. Let me re-read that file's comment: `// Run manually: go test -tags realpi ./internal/agent/piagent/...`. After migration, the file moves to `internal/runtime`, so the correct comment is `./internal/runtime/...`. This is clearly part of \"migrate the test\" (the task says \"真实 pi 冒烟测试（realpi/realds）在无 pi 时跳过\" — migrate these tests). Migrating a test includes updating its doc comment path. So I'll keep the literal grep.\n\nFinal decision: keep literal grep. It's faithful and defensible.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider once more, because there's a subtlety I might be missing: is there any chance the implementer leaves `internal/agent/piagent` as a *package comment* in a NEW file, or the realpi comment gets moved verbatim? The risk is real. But I've decided: faithful to spec. If the grader's own \"doing\" phase for task4 produces the code, that same doing prompt (task4_doing_prompt) would instruct them to update comments. Let me check if there's a task4_doing_prompt that I can read to see what the implementer is told.\n\nActually, there's no task4_doing_prompt in the prompts dir (only task1_doing_prompt.md, task2_doing_prompt.md, task1_testgen_prompt.md, task2_testgen_prompt.md, task4_testgen_prompt.md, ctrl_prompt.md, skill files). The doing prompt for task4 might be generated later. But the plan/task4.md is the spec the implementer follows. It says imports changed; comments not explicitly. \n\nI'll keep it faithful. Moving on.\n\nNow, another thought: my `grep -rn 'internal/agent/piagent' internal/` — after migration, the OLD directory `internal/agent/piagent` will be gone (moved). But grep searches `internal/` recursively including `internal/runtime/`. The migrated files won't contain the old path (they're now package runtime, and their internal comments... wait, executor.go's package comment says \"Package piagent integrates rick...\" — after migration this comment must change to \"Package runtime...\" or be updated. Does that comment contain \"internal/agent/piagent\"? No, it says \"Package piagent\". So grep for \"internal/agent/piagent\" won't match it. Good.\n\nBut wait, the migrated `cli.go`, `executor.go`, `agentdir.go` files: do they contain the string \"internal/agent/piagent\" anywhere? Let me check cur