{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The script runs directly with shebang and produces valid JSON.\n\nNow, let me reflect on whether I've covered all the acceptance criteria from the test method:\n\n1. Normal path: ✅ build (`go build ./cmd/rick`), ✅ `go test ./internal/runtime/...`.\n2. Boundary: ✅ isSessionReady (source + test coverage, behavior via go test), ✅ TestLoadConfig_RuntimeDefault (test name + go test ./internal/config/... + config field + default + normalization).\n3. Exception: ✅ FindBinary error message + TestFindBinary_FallsBackToPathLookup; ✅ grep no residual; ✅ internal/agent interface + internal/actpath retained.\n\nPlus key results: runtime implements agent interface, Runtime interface/piRuntime/Trace/--append-system-prompt, old package gone, migrated source files present.\n\nI think this is comprehensive and faithful.\n\nLet me do a final review of whether there are any subtle issues that could cause a false PASS (test passes when it shouldn't) — the more dangerous failure mode per the self-check guidance. The guidance says: if pass=true but feature not implemented, the test logic is defective.\n\nCurrently pass=false (feature not implemented), which is the correct RED state. Good.\n\nCould there be a scenario where the feature is only partially implemented but my test still passes (false green)? Let me consider:\n- If implementer creates `internal/runtime` with cli.go/executor.go/agentdir.go copied (package renamed) and migrates tests, but forgets isSessionReady — my check `isSessionReady` in source → error. Good.\n- If they forget Runtime interface — `type Runtime interface` → error. Good.\n- If they forget config field — error. Good.\n- If they forget to remove old package — old_go_files → error. Good.\n- If they leave imports in cmd — grep → error. Good.\n- If they forget normalization — `cfg.Runtime` → error. Good.\n\nThe checks are quite specific and would catch partial implementations.\n\nOne concern: what if the implementer names the session readiness function differently than `isSessionReady` (e.g., `isReady`)? The spec explicitly says `isSessionReady`, so this is a valid strict check. Good.\n\nWhat if `Trace` struct fields differ? I only check `type Trace struct` exists, not the fields. That's a reasonable leniency (the exact fields are implementation detail; the spec lists them but I don't need to over-constrain). Acceptable.\n\nWhat if `piRuntime` is named `piRuntime` (exact)? Spec says `piRuntime`. Good.\n\nI'm confident the script is correct and complete.\n\nLet me also make sure I haven't left any temp build dirs that matter. The `tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='rick_task4_build_')` creates dirs in /tmp each run. That's harmless. I could clean up but it's fine.\n\nActually, let me add a small cleanup to avoid accumulating /tmp dirs, for tidiness. It's a minor improvement. I'll wrap the build in a try/finally or just shutil.rmtree at the end. Actually, since the build output dir is only needed during the build check, I can rmtree right after the build check. But if the script is interrupted, it leaks. Not a big deal. I'll add a `shutil.rmtree(build_dir, ignore_errors=True)` at the end of main for tidiness. Actually, to keep it simple and avoid touching working code, I'll leave it. /tmp leaks are negligible and cleaned on reboot.\n\nActually, I realize there might be value in cleaning up to keep the environment clean for repeated test runs in the grading loop. But mkdtemp with unique prefix avoids any collision, so no correctness issue. I'll leave it.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the prompt says the test script should use absolute paths. My script uses `os.path.join(repo_root, ...)` where repo_root is absolute (from find_repo_root). ✅. The subprocess cwd is repo_root (absolute). The `grep` uses relative `internal/` but with cwd=repo_root (absolute), which is fine. Actually the prompt's DON'T says \"使用相对路径（容易出错）\". I use cwd=absolute repo_root and pass `./cmd/rick`, `./internal/runtime/...` etc. as Go package patterns (these are module-relative patterns, not filesystem relative paths — that's how `go` works). The grep uses `internal/` relative to cwd (absolute). This is acceptable and standard. The key file checks all use absolute paths via os.path.join. ✅.\n\nI think everything is in order. Let me write a brief summary.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider one more potential issue with `go test ./internal/runtime/...` in the GREEN phase: the migrated `executor_realpi_smoke_test.go` has `//go:build realpi`. When I run `go test ./internal/runtime/...` WITHOUT the realpi tag, that file is excluded from compilation. But my `concat_files(runtime_test)` reads ALL `_test.go` files including the realpi one (file reading doesn't respect build tags). So the `agent_settled` fixture check (which searches runtime_test_txt) would find `agent_settled` in executor_test.go anyway. And `TestParseStream_NoAgentSettled` is in executor_test.go (no build tag). Good.\n\nAlso, `executor_realds_test.go` (no build tag) contains `agent_settled`? It parses a real deepseek stream. It might or might not contain `agent_settled` literal. Doesn't matter; executor_test.go has it.\n\nOK. Final: I'm confident.\n\nLet me also verify there are no syntax errors by running `python3 -m py_compile`."}}
